19:07.766 --> 19:17.135
[SPEAKER_00]: trickling over the front you know trickling over the dayadar into the back and the back was beautifully blue and crisp and the front was hot wet

19:17.295 --> 19:26.905
[SPEAKER_00]: sticky and smoggy I think that the scene that you're narrating right now is a pretty common scene over here. I fully agree that inversions are

19:26.965 --> 19:37.495
[SPEAKER_01]: thicker, grayer, and darker. Especially in the evenings, when everything starts to settle down, it's a very thick blanket of cloud.

19:38.195 --> 19:49.224
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, true. Well, when we talk about flying conditions between front ridge and back ridge, Eddie, let's say you're a pilot coming into India,

19:50.145 --> 19:58.952
[SPEAKER_01]: and this is one of your first flights, although you, Bob and John, have been smashing it since day one, ever since you arrived, because you had a

19:58.992 --> 20:11.323
[SPEAKER_01]: bit of a skill set. What is the safe yet adventurous way to navigate your first few days? I mean, let's call them acclimatization days, right? So

